{
  "schema_version": "1.0.0",
  "knowledge_version": "1.5.0",
  "provenance": "Project-authored from the WHO ATC classification (C09A, C09C, C07, C08, C03, N02A, C10AA, C02CA, G04CA, R03AC, R03BB) for ingredients marketed in the United States during the MIMIC-IV period. Written by the coding agent on 2026-09-24; pending clinical review. Added 2026-10-18: alpha blockers, beta-2 agonists, short- and long-acting muscarinic antagonists. 1.4.0 (2026-10-27): locally applied dosage forms (ointments, creams, gels, eye and ear drops, nasal, rectal, vaginal and rinse forms) are left out of every class by one catalogue-wide rule over whole label words instead of word lists on twelve ingredients, so the topical diltiazem and nifedipine ointments no longer count as calcium-channel blockers and 'Oint' counts as an ointment; a class lists the forms that are its own route (ophthalmic beta blockers: eye forms; laxatives: rectal forms; opioids: nasal and rectal forms, which are systemic for butorphanol, morphine and hydromorphone). 1.5.0 (2026-10-28): warfarin is in the catalogue as the vitamin K antagonists (B01AA); 'oral anticoagulants' is a class of its own (warfarin and the direct oral anticoagulants) instead of a synonym of the direct oral anticoagulants, which silently left warfarin out, and 'direct anticoagulants' (which also names injectable direct thrombin inhibitors) no longer names the direct oral anticoagulants. A synonym narrower than its class now names a class of its own (地平类: the dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers, without diltiazem and verapamil; stool softener: docusate, not every laxative), and one broader than its class is removed ('glaucoma eye drops' also covers prostaglandin analogues, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and alpha agonists, not only the ophthalmic beta blockers). The direct oral anticoagulants have their Chinese and trade names (阿哌沙班, 达比加群, 依度沙班, 利伐沙班; Eliquis, Pradaxa, Savaysa, Lixiana, Xarelto).",
